# Legion runtime state. Ignored here and not only in a developer's global
# ignore file: a fresh clone would otherwise show run artifacts as untracked,
# and .legion/tasks holds the verbatim task text staged as run evidence, which
# routinely contains prompts nobody intends to publish. .legion/worktrees can
# also carry a full seeded node_modules.
#
# Ignore the CONTENTS, not the directory: .legion also holds repository-owned
# configuration -- sandbox.json, documented in legion-router/README.md, and
# legion-core.json, the declared Legion baseline per AGENTS.md. A blanket
# `.legion/` hid both from `git status`, which is why committing a pin needs
# `git add -f` in the consumer-update workflow.
#
# The directory itself must be un-ignored FIRST. Git does not descend into an
# excluded directory, and a file cannot be re-included once a parent is
# excluded -- so with the documented global `.legion/` exclusion still in a
# developer's config, the negations below would never be reached. This line is
# what makes them effective; repo rules outrank core.excludesFile.
!.legion/
.legion/*
!.legion/sandbox.json
!.legion/legion-core.json

# Claude Code local session state
.claude/

# Local env / secrets — never commit
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
credentials
aws-credentials*
